arXiv:2103.05038v2 [math.AG] 25 Jun 2022</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Hodge cycles and the Leray filtration<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>Partially supported by a grant from the Simons foundation</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Donu Arapura
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Department of Mathematics
<br class="ltx_break">Purdue University
<br class="ltx_break">West Lafayette, IN 47907
<br class="ltx_break">U.S.A.
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a fibered variety, we
pull back the Leray filtration to the Chow group, and use this to
give some criteria for the Hodge and Tate conjectures to hold for
such varieties. We show that the Hodge
conjecture holds for a good desingularization of a self fibre
product of a nonisotrivial elliptic surface under appropriate conditions. We also show that the
Hodge and Tate conjectures hold for natural families of abelian
varieties parameterized by certain Shimura curves.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">2020 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>14C30
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">A2</a>]</cite>, we checked the Hodge and Tate conjectures for the universal family of genus two curves with level structure
by showing the absence of these cycles in a critical piece of the
Leray spectral sequence. In this article, we will to take this idea further.
Given a surjective map of smooth projective varieties <math id="p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq Y</annotation></semantics></math> be the complement of the discriminant and <math id="p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=f^{-1}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=f^{-1}U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then we define a filtration on the rationalized Chow group such that we have a cycle map</p>
<table id="S0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{p,i}:Gr^{i}_{L}CH^{p}(V)\to H^{i}(U,R^{2p-i}f_{*}{\mathbb{Q}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{p,i}:Gr^{i}_{L}CH^{p}(V)\to H^{i}(U,R^{2p-i}f_{*}{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">A1</a>]</cite>, the right side carries a mixed Hodge structure.
We have that <math id="p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{p,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{p,i}</annotation></semantics></math> lands within the space of Hodge cycles. Let
<math id="p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\dim Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\dim Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\dim X-\dim Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\dim X-\dim Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our first theorem is that the Hodge conjecture, in Jannsen’s sense, holds for <math id="p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>,
if <math id="p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{p,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{p,i}</annotation></semantics></math> surjects onto the space of Hodge cycles for all <math id="p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,i)</annotation></semantics></math> in the parallelogram bounded by
the lines <math id="p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=m</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2p</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2p-r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2p-r</annotation></semantics></math>. An analogous statement holds for the Tate conjecture. As a corollary, we show that when <math id="p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a fourfold,
the Hodge conjecture holds for <math id="p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{2,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{2,m}</annotation></semantics></math> surjects onto the space of Hodge cycles. A special case of this was used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">A2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the rest of this article, we turn our attention to particular classes of examples.
Suppose that <math id="p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\lambda}:{\mathcal{E}}_{\lambda}\to Y_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\lambda}:{\mathcal{E}}_{\lambda}\to Y_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of nonisotrivial elliptic surfaces,
such that the “Kodaira-Spencer” map</p>
<table id="S0.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\kappa_{\lambda}:Gr^{1}_{F}H^{1}(U_{\lambda},R^{1}f_{\lambda*}{\mathbb{C}})\to Gr^{0}_{F}H^{1}(U_{\lambda},R^{1}f_{\lambda*}{\mathbb{C}})\otimes\Omega^{1}_{\lambda}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\lambda}:Gr^{1}_{F}H^{1}(U_{\lambda},R^{1}f_{\lambda*}{\mathbb{C}})\to Gr^{0}_{F}H^{1}(U_{\lambda},R^{1}f_{\lambda*}{\mathbb{C}})\otimes\Omega^{1}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p2.2" class="ltx_p">is injective somewhere. Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2. Fibre products of elliptic surfaces ‣ Hodge cycles and the Leray filtration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> shows
that the Hodge conjecture holds for the complement <math id="p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{\lambda}\subset{\mathcal{E}}_{\lambda}\times_{Y_{\lambda}}\ldots\times_{Y_{\lambda}}{\mathcal{E}}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\lambda}\subset{\mathcal{E}}_{\lambda}\times_{Y_{\lambda}}\ldots\times_{Y_{\lambda}}{\mathcal{E}}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>
of the set of singular fibres, when <math id="p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is very general.
For a zero dimensional family, injectivity of <math id="p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> holds
if and only if the elliptic surface is extremal in the sense
that the Picard number is maximal and the Mordell-Weil rank is zero (lemma <a href="#S2.Thmlemma6" title="Lemma 2.6. ‣ 2. Fibre products of elliptic surfaces ‣ Hodge cycles and the Leray filtration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>).
Elliptic modular surfaces are extremal, so we recover a result of Gordon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">G</a>]</cite> that theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2. Fibre products of elliptic surfaces ‣ Hodge cycles and the Leray filtration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>
holds for such surfaces. Our theorem also holds for certain
families of nonextremal elliptic K3 surfaces constructed by Dolgachev
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Do</a>]</cite> and Hoyt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">H</a>]</cite>. Gordon’s argument for elliptic
modular surfaces does not generalize. Our proof is different, and is based on analyzing
<math id="p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{p,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{p,i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0,1</annotation></semantics></math>. The map <math id="p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{p,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{p,0}</annotation></semantics></math> is shown to surject onto the space of Hodge
cycles by the Künneth formula and invariant theory. On the other hand for very general <math id="p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, we will show <math id="p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{p,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{p,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivially surjective in the sense that there are no Hodge cycles on the target. This will be deduced by showing that injectivity of <math id="p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>
implies injectivity of the Kodaira-Spencer map</p>
<table id="S0.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr^{p}_{F}H^{1}(U_{\lambda},R^{2p-1}F_{\lambda*}{\mathbb{C}})\to Gr^{p-1}_{F}H^{1}(U_{\lambda},R^{2p-1}F_{\lambda*}{\mathbb{C}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{p}_{F}H^{1}(U_{\lambda},R^{2p-1}F_{\lambda*}{\mathbb{C}})\to Gr^{p-1}_{F}H^{1}(U_{\lambda},R^{2p-1}F_{\lambda*}{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\lambda}:V_{\lambda}\to Y_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\lambda}:V_{\lambda}\to Y_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is the structure map.</p>
</div>
<div id="p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p3.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a Shimura curve associated to a quaternion
division algebra
split at exactly one real place over a totally real field. The curve
<math id="p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as a moduli space of abelian varieties with some extra structure.
In particular, for sufficiently high level, <math id="p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> carries a natural family of abelian varietes <math id="p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{A}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{A}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3. Families of abelian varieties over a Shimura curve ‣ Hodge cycles and the Leray filtration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> shows that the Hodge
and Tate conjectures
holds for a fibre product <math id="p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X=\mathcal{A}\times_{Y}\ldots\times_{Y}\mathcal{A}\xrightarrow{F}Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝐹</mo></mover><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\mathcal{A}\times_{Y}\ldots\times_{Y}\mathcal{A}\xrightarrow{F}Y</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof again involves checking surjectivity
of <math id="p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{p,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{p,i}</annotation></semantics></math> onto the space of Hodge cycles. When <math id="p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, this follows from invariant theory. When <math id="p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
work of Viehweg and Zuo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">VZ</a>]</cite> is used to show that in fact</p>
<table id="S0.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{p}_{F}H^{1}(Y,R^{2p-1}F_{*}{\mathbb{C}})=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{p}_{F}H^{1}(Y,R^{2p-1}F_{*}{\mathbb{C}})=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p3.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore <math id="p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{p,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{p,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is again trivially surjective.</p>
</div>
<div id="p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p4.1" class="ltx_p">My thanks to the referee catching a serious error in the first version of this paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Hodge cycles and the Leray filtration</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\MHS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>MHS</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\MHS</annotation></semantics></math> denote the category of rational mixed Hodge structures, and
<math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\MHS^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mo>MHS</mo><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\MHS^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> the full subcategory of polarizable Hodge structures.
We note that mixed Hodge structures of geometric origin in fact lie in
<math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\MHS^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mo>MHS</mo><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\MHS^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the category of pure polarizable Hodge structures is semisimple
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">B</a>]</cite>.
Given a mixed Hodge structure <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, set</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hodge(H):=Hom_{\MHS}({\mathbb{Q}}(0),H)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mo>MHS</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hodge(H):=Hom_{\MHS}({\mathbb{Q}}(0),H)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The following facts will often be used without comment below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmlemma1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is an injection</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hodge(H)\hookrightarrow\Hodge(Gr^{W}_{0}H)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hodge(H)\hookrightarrow\Hodge(Gr^{W}_{0}H)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is an isomorphism if </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has nonnegative weight, i.e. </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-1}H=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-1}H=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hodge(-)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hodge(-)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an exact functor on the category of polarizable
mixed Hodge structures of nonnegative weight.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}\to H_{2}\to H_{3}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}\to H_{2}\to H_{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is an exact sequence of mixed Hodge structures, such
that </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is polarizable with nonnegative weight, then</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hodge(H_{1})\to\Hodge(H_{2})\to\Hodge(H_{3})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hodge(H_{1})\to\Hodge(H_{2})\to\Hodge(H_{3})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is exact.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:H_{1}\to H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:H_{1}\to H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a morphism of polarizable Hodge
structures, such that </span><math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{0}_{F}H_{1}\to Gr_{F}^{0}H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{0}_{F}H_{1}\to Gr_{F}^{0}H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is surjective
(resp. injective)
then </span><math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hodge(H_{1})\to\Hodge(H_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hodge(H_{1})\to\Hodge(H_{2})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is surjective (resp. injective).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first item follows from strictness of the weight filtration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">D3</a>, thm 2.3.5]</cite>.
The second follows from (1) and the semisimplicity of the category of
pure polarizable Hodge structures. Statement (3) follows from (1) and
(2), and the observation that subobjects and quotients of <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are
also polarizable with nonnegative weight.
Given <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:H_{1}\to H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:H_{1}\to H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> inducing
a surjective map <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{0}_{F}H_{1}\to Gr_{F}^{0}H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{0}_{F}H_{1}\to Gr_{F}^{0}H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{3}=\coker\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">coker</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{3}=\coker\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\coker(\Hodge(H_{1})\to\Hodge(H_{2}))=\Hodge(H_{3})\subseteq Gr_{F}^{0}H_{3}=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">coker</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\coker(\Hodge(H_{1})\to\Hodge(H_{2}))=\Hodge(H_{3})\subseteq Gr_{F}^{0}H_{3}=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">The injectivity part of (4) is similar.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic variety, let <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CH_{p}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CH_{p}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CH^{p}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CH^{p}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>)
denote the Chow group of <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional (resp. codimension <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>)
cycles tensored with <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
Borel-Moore homology <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> carries a mixed Hodge structure dual
to the one on
compactly supported cohomology <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}^{i}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}^{i}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have a cycle map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box_{p}:CH_{p}(X)\to H_{2p}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box_{p}:CH_{p}(X)\to H_{2p}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">such that the image lands in
<math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hodge(H_{2p}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})(-p))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hodge(H_{2p}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})(-p))</annotation></semantics></math>, c.f. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">F1</a>, chap 19]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">J</a>, §5, §7]</cite>. We will usually write <math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Z]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Z]</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box_{p}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box_{p}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Following Jannsen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">J</a>]</cite>, we say that the Hodge conjecture holds
for <math id="S1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> if the cycle map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CH_{p}(X)\to\Hodge(H_{2p}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})(-p))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CH_{p}(X)\to\Hodge(H_{2p}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})(-p))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">is surjective for
all <math id="S1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, we can apply Poincaré duality to identify this with the cycle map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{p}:CH^{p}(X)\to\Hodge(H^{2p}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})(p))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{p}:CH^{p}(X)\to\Hodge(H^{2p}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})(p))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">We recall that <math id="S1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded ring homomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let us now suppose that <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a
finitely generated field of characteristic <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{K}=Gal(\bar{K}/K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{K}=Gal(\bar{K}/K)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that
<math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{X}=X\times_{\Spec K}\bar{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">Spec</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{X}=X\times_{\Spec K}\bar{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, define the space of Tate cycles
in <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Tate(H)=\sum_{L/K\text{ finite}}H^{G_{L}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Tate</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> finite</mtext></mrow></munder><msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Tate(H)=\sum_{L/K\text{ finite}}H^{G_{L}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Fix a prime <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>.
Again following Jannsen, we say that Tate’s conjecture holds for <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> if
the cycle map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box_{p,\ell}:CH_{p}(\bar{X})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}_{\ell}\to\Tate(H_{2p}(\bar{X}_{et},{\mathbb{Q}}_{\ell})(-p))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Tate</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box_{p,\ell}:CH_{p}(\bar{X})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}_{\ell}\to\Tate(H_{2p}(\bar{X}_{et},{\mathbb{Q}}_{\ell})(-p))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">is surjective for each <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, this is equivalent to the more familiar statement
that the cycle map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{p}_{\ell}:CH^{p}(\bar{X})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}_{\ell}\to\Tate(H^{2p}(\bar{X}_{et},{\mathbb{Q}}_{\ell})(p))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Tate</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{p}_{\ell}:CH^{p}(\bar{X})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}_{\ell}\to\Tate(H^{2p}(\bar{X}_{et},{\mathbb{Q}}_{\ell})(p))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">is surjective for each <math id="S1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. We note that <math id="S1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box^{p}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box^{p}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> are compatible
under the Artin comparison isomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We note that Jannsen’s form of the Hodge conjecture follows from the
usual Hodge conjecture, so it is not stronger. More precisely, we have:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Jannsen)<span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the Hodge conjecture holds for a desingularization of a
compactification of <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then it holds for <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows from the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">J</a>, thm 7.9]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmlemma2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> be Zariski open, and <math id="S1.Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=X-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=X-U</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S1.Thmlemma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and the Hodge conjecture holds for <math id="S1.Thmlemma2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmlemma2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, then it holds for <math id="S1.Thmlemma2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the Hodge conjecture holds for <math id="S1.Thmlemma2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then it holds for <math id="S1.Thmlemma2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have a localization sequence</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2p}(Z)\to H_{2p}(X)\to H_{2p}(U)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2p}(Z)\to H_{2p}(X)\to H_{2p}(U)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes Borel-Moore homology.
Projectivity of <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> implies that, after twisting by <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}(-p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}(-p)</annotation></semantics></math>, the weight of the first term
becomes nonnegative. So we deduce
from lemma <a href="#S1.Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1.1. ‣ 1. Hodge cycles and the Leray filtration ‣ Hodge cycles and the Leray filtration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> that</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hodge(H_{2p}(Z)(-p))\to\Hodge(H_{2p}(X)(-p))\to\Hodge(H_{2p}(U)(-p))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Hodge</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hodge(H_{2p}(Z)(-p))\to\Hodge(H_{2p}(X)(-p))\to\Hodge(H_{2p}(U)(-p))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">is exact.
By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">F1</a>, §1.8, chap 19]</cite>, this fits into a larger
commutative diagram with exact rows</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.Ex14.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="66.92" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-55.847653581242646px" version="1.1" viewBox="65.14 55.85 508.34 66.92" width="508.34"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 65.14255116717258 66.91725369193864) translate(0,3.46) translate(65.14,0)"><g transform="translate(-29.7,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.69em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="51.1"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{CH_{p}(Z)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{CH_{p}(Z)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 29.700630117257766 -0.27673578005007266 L 29.700630117257766 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(133.79,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 29.700630117257766 -0.27673578005007266 L 133.79329366276204 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 133.79329366276204 -0.27673578005007266 L 133.79329366276204 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -11.569253138458645 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.569253138458645" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(0,-22.14) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-2.09)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.52em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="7.19"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(0,-33.21)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -33.208800332088 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.569253138458645" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -33.208800332088 L 0.27673578005007266 -33.208800332088" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(133.79,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.85em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="53.21"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{CH_{p}(X)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{CH_{p}(X)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 195.30848841853899 -0.27673578005007266 L 195.30848841853899 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(299.4,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 195.30848841853899 -0.27673578005007266 L 299.4011730776299 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 299.4011730776299 -0.27673578005007266 L 299.4011730776299 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 164.27415526060045 -11.569253138458645 L 164.82762682070057 -11.569253138458645" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(164.55,0) translate(0,-33.21)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 164.27415526060045 -33.208800332088 L 164.82762682070057 -11.569253138458645" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 164.27415526060045 -33.208800332088 L 164.82762682070057 -33.208800332088" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(299.4,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.73em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="51.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{CH_{p}(U)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{CH_{p}(U)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 359.32317881295836 -0.27673578005007266 L 359.32317881295836 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(427.98,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 359.32317881295836 -0.27673578005007266 L 427.9739213085478 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 427.9739213085478 -0.27673578005007266 L 427.9739213085478 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 329.08544016524405 -11.569253138458645 L 329.6389117